course: dictionary
lesson: 13047
title: Fascinating and fascinated
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how something makes a person feel. «Fascinating» is one of them. Something fascinating is so interesting that you want to keep looking, listening or learning. «The volcano film was fascinating, so nobody wanted to switch it off.» The sentence tells you the film was gripping, and the end of the sentence shows its effect: nobody wanted to stop watching.
explain: Look at the word family to help you. Fascinate is the verb, meaning to catch and hold someone's interest. Fascinated describes the person who feels it: «Mia was fascinated by the ants in the garden.» Mia is the one who feels interested, and the ants are what fascinates her.
explain: Use the swap check. «The fascinating book kept me reading past bedtime.» Put in very interesting instead of fascinating: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. The clue reading past bedtime shows how gripping the book was.
fact: Fascinating means extremely interesting, so that you want to keep looking, listening or learning.
fact: «Fascinating» is an adjective: it describes a thing, as in «a fascinating fact».
fact: Fascinate is the verb, and fascinates, fascinated and fascinating are its other forms.
fact: A person is «fascinated by» something, and that thing is «fascinating».
fact: Teachers often use fascinating to make a topic sound exciting.
exercise: «The science lesson about bees was fascinating.» What does fascinating mean? | *Extremely interesting | Loud and noisy | Very boring and long | Cold and wet
exercise: «Sam was fascinated by the shiny beetle on the path.» What does fascinated by mean? | *Completely interested in | Scared of | Tired of | Finished with
exercise: «The fascinating book kept Ruby reading all night.» Find and copy one word that describes the book. | *fascinating | night | kept | Ruby
test: «The museum had a fascinating display of old coins.» Which word tells you the display was interesting? | coins | display | museum | *fascinating
test: Dad says the film was fascinating. What does he mean? | It was too short to enjoy | He did not see it at all | It made him fall asleep | *It held his attention and he wanted to keep watching
exam: Which sentence uses fascinating correctly? | The door fascinating was shut for the night. | She fascinating ran to catch the bus. | «The fascinating window was cold and wet.» | *«The fascinating story made the whole class lean forward.»
